,198,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSimplify $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo simplify the expression \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ\\), we will use trigonometric identities and properties of complementary angles. Let's proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, recall that \\(\\cos 70^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 50^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ\\). So, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the product-to-sum identities and the identity for \\(\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\sin 40^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"ad4332b436a5a6dc977ff7d73b9cdc78b11bb725f99677af01da4df57059f566"},"seconds":58.209381341934204,"seed":186021987540501677,"stop":"eos","text":"To proceed, we will use the product-to-sum formulas to simplify \\(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\\). The product-to-sum formula for sine is:\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(A - B) - \\cos(A + B)]\n\\]\n\nApplying this to \\(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(40^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos(40^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ]\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\nDistribute \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the product-to-sum formula for \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(10^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(10^\\circ - 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos(-10^\\circ)]\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos(-10^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSubstitute this back into the expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\right) - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplify the terms:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{3}{16} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThe terms involving \\(\\cos 10^\\circ\\) cancel out, leaving:\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{16}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To proceed, we will use the product-to-sum formulas to simplify \\(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\\). The product-to-sum formula for sine is:\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(A - B) - \\cos(A + B)]\n\\]\n\nApplying this to \\(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(40^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos(40^\\circ + 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ]\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\nDistribute \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the product-to-sum formula for \\(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(10^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(10^\\circ - 20^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos(-10^\\circ)]\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos(-10^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\cos 10^\\circ \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSubstitute this back into the expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\right) - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplify the terms:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{3}{16} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cos 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThe terms involving \\(\\cos 10^\\circ\\) cancel out, leaving:\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{16}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified value is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:405"}
